---
params: [name: String, moduleName: String, library: Bool]
---
# <%= name %>

A Kex package.

## Develop

```sh
tey install   # fetch dependencies
tey build     # compile into ebin/
tey test      # run spec/*.spec.kex
```

<% if !library %>
## Run it

```sh
tey install
tey run
```

<% end %>
## Add it to your project

In your own package's `package.kex`:

```kex
tey("<%= name %>", git: "https://github.com/you/<%= name %>", tag: "~> 0.1")
```

Then `tey install`. The tag is the release, and `~> 0.1` takes the
newest `0.1.x` — Tey resolves it against this repository's tags.

<% if library %>
## Use it

```kex
using <%= moduleName %>

main(args) do
  IO.printLine(<%= moduleName %>.greeting("world"))
end
```

## Release it

```sh
git tag v0.1.0 && git push origin v0.1.0
```

Keep the tag in step with `version` in `package.kex`.

<% end %>
## Your own commands

A package can add commands to Tey. Declare them in `package.kex`:

```kex
command("bench", run: "script/bench.kex", description: "Run the benchmarks")
command("fmt", run: "kex --format src", description: "Format the sources")
```

They run as `tey bench` and `tey fmt` from anywhere in the package, are
listed under **Package commands** in `tey help`, and show up as tasks in
editors that ask Tey what this project can do. A `run:` whose first word
ends in `.kex` is run by Kex; anything else is a shell command line.

Built-in names (`build`, `test`, `run`, ...) cannot be redeclared, so
`tey test` means the same thing in every package.
